---
// Shared page header: pulsing-dot eyebrow, giant title, drifting dot-halftone
// texture, and an intro paragraph beside the title (≥760px) with a hairline
// divider. Originally built for the Éditions overview (sketch-012 F1,
// quick-260728-lbh/nj7/ok5). Extracted so Contact (quick-260728-ek0 follow-up)
// renders the EXACT same markup/CSS instead of a hand-synced copy — two
// separate implementations of "the same" header drifted out of alignment
// (title/eyebrow position, halftone coverage, hairline position) the moment
// either one changed.
//
// The halftone's -700px bleed (needed for its edge-less fade) is
// deliberately NOT clipped vertically — Éditions wants the dot field to
// visibly extend past the header box, per its original design. Horizontal
// bleed used to be clipped at `.page-title-header`'s own box edge via
// `overflow-x: clip` (see that rule's own comment for why a site-wide
// `overflow-x: hidden` on html/body was tried and reverted — 2026-07-29 —
// it broke `position: sticky` on About's pinned exhibition photo and
// DetailHero's pinned reveal, both unrelated pages that happen to share the
// same document). As of Phase 19 (UI-01) the clip is gone and the halftone
// instead reaches the TRUE viewport edge via viewport-relative geometry —
// see that rule's own comment for the new containment model. Do not move
// any overflow containment for this component back up to BaseLayout, and
// never add an `overflow-x` rule targeting `html` or `body`.
interface Props {
  eyebrow?: string;
  heading: string;
  headingId?: string;
  intro: string;
}

const { eyebrow = 'Atelier Jacqueline Suzanne', heading, headingId, intro } = Astro.props;
---

<header class="page-title-header">
  <div class="page-title-header__halftone" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="page-title-header__titleblock">
    <p class="page-title-header__eyebrow">{eyebrow}</p>
    <h1 id={headingId}>{heading}</h1>
  </div>
  <p class="page-title-header__intro">{intro}</p>
  <div class="page-title-header__divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>
</header>

<style>
  /* History: this rule used to carry `overflow-x: clip`, which clipped the
     halftone's horizontal -700px bleed at .page-title-header's own box
     edge — that was overflowing every consumer page's document width at
     >=760px (live CI failure, 2026-07-29). A site-wide `overflow-x: hidden`
     on html/body was tried FIRST and reverted the same day because it
     broke `position: sticky` on About's pinned exhibition photo and
     DetailHero's pinned reveal, both unrelated pages that happen to share
     the same document — never move overflow containment for this
     component back up to html/body or BaseLayout.

     Phase 19 (UI-01): the component-level `overflow-x: clip` was REMOVED.
     It fixed the overflow bug but hard-cut the halftone at the content
     column's edge instead of letting it bleed to the true browser edge —
     that hard cut was itself the visual defect UI-01 reports. Measured at
     planning time: a clipping ancestor and a child full-bleed are mutually
     exclusive (a clipped .page-title-header suppressed the halftone's
     bleed entirely, even though the halftone's own box measured full
     viewport width) — so "keep the clip as a safety net" is not viable.
     Containment is now geometric instead of clip-based: the halftone's own
     box (see .page-title-header__halftone below) is sized to exactly one
     viewport width via a viewport-relative centering formula, so it has no
     horizontal overflow to propagate to the document in the first place. Known
     residual: viewport units are scrollbar-inclusive, so on a platform
     that draws space-taking scrollbars (Windows/Linux Chrome, or macOS
     with "Show scroll bars: Always") the halftone's right edge can land up
     to ~half a scrollbar width (7-8px) past the client edge and produce a
     small horizontal scrollbar; headless CI (overlay scrollbars) cannot
     observe this, so it's checked during end-of-phase human verification
     — if seen, add a guard term to the halftone's RIGHT inset (e.g.
     `right: calc(50% - 50vw + 8px)`). The axe `scrollable-region-focusable`
     concern that motivated `clip` over `hidden` no longer applies, because
     nothing on this element is clipped anymore.

     `position: relative` + `isolation: isolate` stay: `position: relative`
     makes this element the halftone's containing block (required for the
     viewport-relative centering trick below to resolve against the
     viewport rather than some further ancestor), and `isolation: isolate`
     keeps the halftone's `z-index: -1` layer contained to this component's
     own stacking context. */
  .page-title-header {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-lg);
  }
  @media (min-width: 760px) {
    .page-title-header {
      grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
      align-items: end;
      column-gap: var(--space-2xl);
    }
  }

  .page-title-header__titleblock {
    position: relative;
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 1;
    /* min-width: 0 overrides the CSS Grid default (min-width: auto), which
       otherwise refuses to let a grid item shrink below its content's
       min-content size. A single unbreakable word (e.g. "Contact",
       "Éditions") at the h1's 64px font-size floor has a min-content width
       wider than the 320px viewport's content column — without this, the
       grid track (and with it .page-title-header itself) was forced to
       grow past the viewport instead of letting the h1 wrap, which is what
       .page-title-header's old overflow-x: clip was silently masking.
       Found and fixed while removing that clip for UI-01 (Rule 1: the bug
       was pre-existing, only ever hidden by the clip this plan removes). */
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .page-title-header__eyebrow {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    width: fit-content;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--text-label-size);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    line-height: var(--text-label-leading);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    transition: color 0.35s ease;
  }
  .page-title-header__eyebrow::before {
    content: '';
    width: 8px;
    height: 8px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    display: inline-block;
    animation: page-title-header-eyebrow-pulse 2.2s ease-in-out infinite;
    transition: background-color 0.35s ease;
  }
  @keyframes page-title-header-eyebrow-pulse {
    0%, 100% { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1); }
    50% { opacity: 0.4; transform: scale(0.7); }
  }

  .page-title-header h1 {
    margin: var(--space-md) 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(64px, 9vw, 140px);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    /* No white-space: nowrap — the 64px font-size floor doesn't shrink
       further on narrow phones, so a nowrap title longer than ~"Contact"
       (e.g. About's "À propos"/"About") overflowed the viewport
       horizontally at 320-360px (live CI failure, 2026-07-29). Wrapping to
       2 lines on narrow viewports is the correct behavior, not the nowrap
       single-line-at-any-cost that was here before. */
    display: inline-block;
    max-width: 100%;
    /* Multi-word titles (About's "À propos") wrap fine on their own word
       boundary. Single-word titles ("Contact", "Éditions") have no
       break-point to wrap on, so their min-content width can still exceed
       the 320px viewport's content column even after the fix above — this
       break-word safety net lets a single overlong word wrap mid-word as a
       last resort, only if it would otherwise overflow. Same discovery as
       the .page-title-header__titleblock min-width fix above: this was
       always a latent bug, only ever masked by .page-title-header's old
       overflow-x: clip. */
    overflow-wrap: break-word;
    transition: color 0.35s ease;
  }

  .page-title-header__intro {
    grid-column: 1;
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: #3a3a3a;
    max-width: 46ch;
    transition: color 0.35s ease, border-color 0.35s ease;
  }
  @media (min-width: 760px) {
    .page-title-header__intro {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 1; /* explicit row — otherwise auto-placement pushes this to row 2 once .page-title-header__divider explicitly claims column 2/row 1 */
      padding-bottom: 0.3em;
      padding-left: var(--space-lg);
    }
  }

  /* The vertical divider used to be border-left on the intro paragraph
     itself — sized to that paragraph's own content height. That meant a
     shorter intro (Contact: 2 lines) drew a visibly shorter, lower-starting
     line than a longer one (Éditions: 4 lines), even with byte-identical
     CSS. Decoupled into its own grid item with align-self:stretch so the
     line always spans the full row height, on both pages, regardless of
     how many lines the intro text happens to wrap to. */
  .page-title-header__divider {
    display: none;
  }
  @media (min-width: 760px) {
    .page-title-header__divider {
      display: block;
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 1;
      justify-self: start;
      align-self: end; /* bottom-anchored, matching the row's own align-items:end — same baseline as the title block and intro text */
      height: 120px;
      width: var(--border-hairline);
      background: var(--color-border);
      transition: background-color 0.35s ease;
    }
  }

  .page-title-header__halftone {
    position: absolute;
    top: -700px;
    bottom: -700px;
    /* The left/right inset below centers this box on the viewport and
       sizes it to exactly one viewport width (measured: rect exactly
       0 -> document.documentElement.clientWidth, zero horizontal overflow,
       in both Chromium and WebKit at 1280 and 1920px) — geometrically
       correct containment, replacing the -700px fixed offset the
       overflow-x: clip on .page-title-header used to have to cut off. The
       `50%` term resolves against .page-title-header's own box (its
       containing block, via position: relative above), not the viewport,
       which is why that rule must keep position: relative. */
    left: calc(50% - 50vw);
    right: calc(50% - 50vw);
    background-image: radial-gradient(rgba(26, 26, 26, 0.16) 1.4px, transparent 1.6px);
    background-size: 9px 9px;
    /* Mask focal-point offset: the dot cloud must keep emanating from
       .page-title-header's own top-right corner, not slide toward the
       middle of the window now that the halftone's own right edge is the
       viewport's right edge. The offset below is the real distance from
       the viewport's right edge to this header's right edge — half the
       viewport width, minus half of whichever is narrower (the viewport or
       the editorial content max-width), plus the editorial page's inline
       padding. Depends on the three consumer containers (.contact-page,
       .about-page, .editions-list) all sharing
       --editorial-page-max/--editorial-page-padding-inline
       (BaseLayout.astro). Verified arithmetically at planning time: at
       1280px, 640 - 590 + 48 = 98px, matching the header box's measured
       right edge (x=1182, 98px from the viewport edge); at 1920px,
       960 - 590 + 48 = 418px, matching x=1502. */
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(circle 640px at right calc(50vw - min(100vw, var(--editorial-page-max)) / 2 + var(--editorial-page-padding-inline)) top 700px, black 0%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.72) 18%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.42) 40%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.18) 65%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05) 85%, transparent 100%);
    mask-image: radial-gradient(circle 640px at right calc(50vw - min(100vw, var(--editorial-page-max)) / 2 + var(--editorial-page-padding-inline)) top 700px, black 0%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.72) 18%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.42) 40%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.18) 65%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05) 85%, transparent 100%);
    z-index: -1;
    pointer-events: none;
    display: none;
    opacity: 0;
    transition: filter 0.3s ease;
    animation: page-title-header-halftone-fade-in 0.6s ease 0.3s forwards, page-title-header-halftone-drift 10s linear infinite;
  }
  @media (min-width: 760px) {
    .page-title-header__halftone {
      display: block;
    }
  }
  @keyframes page-title-header-halftone-fade-in {
    to { opacity: 1; }
  }
  @keyframes page-title-header-halftone-drift {
    from { background-position: 0 0; }
    to { background-position: 90px 45px; }
  }
  .page-title-header:hover .page-title-header__halftone {
    animation-duration: 0.6s, 4s;
    filter: contrast(1.35) brightness(0.85);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .page-title-header__halftone {
      animation: none;
      opacity: 1 !important;
    }
    .page-title-header__eyebrow::before {
      animation: none;
    }
  }
</style>
